Geriatric & Frailty
Unexplained change in bowel habits, Rectal bleeding/iron deficiency anaemia, Unintentional weight loss, Irregular friable mass on colonoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-010
Reason for Visit: Severe, progressive colicky abdominal pain accompanied by repeated vomiting and a cessation of normal bowel movements.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, escalating abdominal pain and intractable vomiting, alongside a history of altered bowel habits. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate for acute partial or complete mechanical bowel obstruction, preventing ischemic bowel, perforation, or severe metabolic derangement prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach is cramping so violently it takes my breath away, I can't keep any water down, and I haven't passed gas or stool in two days."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an insidious progressive change in normal bowel habits over the past four months, transitioning to new-onset persistent constipation. They intermittently noticed visible bright red rectal bleeding mixed with the stool. Over the last three months, they have experienced profound anorexia and unintentional significant weight loss of approximately 8 kilograms. They recently noted severe lethargy consistent with unexplained iron deficiency anaemia. Over the last 24 hours, the symptoms progressed rapidly to severe, progressive colicky abdominal pain occurring in waves. They describe tenesmus and a persistent sensation of incomplete rectal emptying. Today, this culminated in nausea and recurrent, large-volume bilious vomiting, prompting the emergency call.
Functional Status: Previously independent but fatigued; currently bedbound and writhing in pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe dehydration; profound psychological distress regarding the underlying cause of the weight loss.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 110 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Appears cachectic, pale, diaphoretic, and in severe acute distress, frequently changing positions in bed.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals visible distension and high-pitched, hyperactive 'tinkling' bowel sounds on auscultation. Palpation demonstrates generalized tenderness with a palpable firm, fixed abdominal mass in the left lower quadrant. There is voluntary guarding but no rigid board-like abdomen or rebound tenderness to suggest frank perforation. Percussion is highly tympanic. Digital rectal examination reveals an empty rectal vault with streaks of altered blood on the glove. No hepatomegaly or clinical jaundice is noted currently. (Direct visualization of an irregular, friable ulcerating mass on full colonoscopy is an anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding).
Environmental Observations: A bucket next to the bed contains large volumes of bilious emesis.
Medication Reconciliation: Not currently taking opiate analgesia or anti-motility agents that could explain the severe constipation.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Colorectal Cancer presenting as acute mechanical large bowel obstruction - Status: Acute Emergency
Differentials:

Severe Diverticulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of high fever, localized peritonism, or history of acute, inflammatory left lower quadrant pain prior to this chronic progressive functional decline and weight loss.

Severe Faecal Impaction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Empty rectal vault on digital examination, presence of a discrete palpable mass rather than generalized faecal loading, and the presence of 'red flag' symptoms like bleeding and profound weight loss.

Acute Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of diarrhoea, presence of severe colicky pain, visible abdominal distension, high-pitched bowel sounds, and profound chronicity of underlying symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Medical Emergency)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with classic features of an acute mechanical bowel obstruction (colicky pain, distension, vomiting, absolute constipation) superimposed on a highly suspicious history of progressive bowel habit changes, rectal bleeding, and significant weight loss. Telephone triage accurately identified an acute, life-threatening abdominal crisis. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would unequivocally risk catastrophic bowel ischemia, perforation, severe sepsis, and likely death. This urgent face-to-face assessment confirmed the presence of a pathological obstruction and a palpable mass, mandating immediate emergency medical intervention and securing rapid transport to a surgical facility.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate escalation to emergency services. Administered a single dose of intramuscular antiemetic (metoclopramide 10mg, cautiously given the obstruction) to mitigate immediate vomiting distress while awaiting transport. Nil by mouth status strictly enforced. Detailed clinical handover prepared for the emergency department receiving team.
Positive Behavioral Change: (Not applicable in an acute, life-threatening emergency requiring immediate transfer; focus is entirely on stabilizing and expediting transport).
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Maintained patient in a comfortable position with an emesis basin while awaiting the ambulance.
Follow-up: Immediate handover to Emergency Department General Surgical team.

